timestamp=2026-11-04T13:15:26.979Z level=INFO run=f66adc04 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/synthetic_stroke_data.csv')\nmask_valid = (df['avg_glucose_level'] >= 20) & (df['avg_glucose_level'] <= 800) & \\\n             (df['bmi'] >= 10) & (df['bmi'] <= 60) & \\\n             (df['age'] <= 120)\ndf = df[mask_valid].copy()\n\ncol = 'avg_glucose_level'\nq1 = df[col].quantile(0.25)\nq3 = df[col].quantile(0.75)\niqr = q3 - q1\nlower = q1 - 3*iqr\nupper = q3 + 3*iqr\nout_mask = (df[col] < lower) | (df[col] > upper)\n\n# apply rule\n# for each observation identified as outlier by IQR?\n# check: if 50% or more of other observations sharing the same values correspond to stroke cases, not outlier\n# also: if > 20% but < 50%? rule says: \"If 50% or more of other observations sharing the same values correspond to stroke cases, they are not considered outliers. \n# If more than 20% of other observations sharing the same values correspond to stroke cases, a base-2 logarithmic transformation should be applied and the above check should be performed again.\"\n\n# let's interpret carefully\n# For observations in avg_glucose_level identified as outliers:\n#   - If 50% or more of other observations sharing the same values correspond to stroke cases, they are not considered outliers.\n#   - If more than 20% of other observations sharing the same values correspond to stroke cases, a base-2 logarithmic transformation should be applied and the above check should be performed again.\n# So maybe: first check prop of stroke among all observations with the same value (excluding the obs itself? or just \"other observations sharing the same values\"). \n# But there may be 1 obs per value. Also the phrase says \"50% or more of other observations sharing the same values...\" Maybe if total count of obs with that exact value > 1, look at others. If count == 1, then \"other observations\" is empty? Or prop of stroke in all same-value obs is considered. \n# In our data, each outlier value appears once. So for a single observation with value v, \"other observations sharing the same values\" = 0. Then 50% of 0? That’s problematic. \n# Or maybe compare: for the group defined by that value v, what fraction of observations in the dataset that have v are stroke cases? \n# The rule as stated is a bit ambiguous. But likely: among all rows with the same avg_glucose_level value v (not just outliers), if >= 50% have stroke == 1, then those observations are not outliers (even if IQR flags them). If > 20% and < 50% have stroke, apply log2 and re-check using the same logic? Or re-check the outlier status after transformation? \n# Also the second part says \"a base-2 logarithmic transformation should be applied and the above check should be performed again.\" - \"above check\" meaning the 50% rule check? Or the whole outlier identification process? \n\n# Let us look up typical contest-style interpretations. Another reading: for observations flagged as outliers in avg_glucose_level, inspect their \"peer group\" (other observations sharing the same value). \n# If >= 50% of other observations in that peer group are stroke cases, then this outlier observation is not actually an outlier (it belongs to a high-risk cluster at that exact glucose level). \n# If > 20% (so 20% < prop <= 50%?) are stroke cases, then apply log2 transform and repeat the above check - meaning apply log2 and then again check this peer-group-based condition? Or re-run outlier detection on transformed scale and see? \n# Also the rule says \"they are not considered outliers\" - \"they\" refers to the observations with that glucose value? \n# This is important. Let us re-read: \"For observations in avg_glucose_level identified as outliers: If 50% or more of other observations sharing the same values correspond to stroke cases, they are not considered outliers. If more than 20% of other observations sharing the same values correspond to stroke cases, a base-2 logarithmic transformation should be applied and the above check should be performed again.\" \n# So for each such observation (outlier), look at other observations that have the same avg_glucose_level value as this observation. Among those \"other observations\", what % correspond to stroke cases? \n# If that percentage >= 50%, then \"they\" (these outlier observations) are not considered outliers. \n# If > 20% (i.e., 20% < pct <= 50%? because >= 50% is the previous case), then apply log2 transform and perform \"the above check\" again - the above check being the 50% rule? Or check again whether they’re outliers under some criterion? \n# Maybe the process is: identify outliers via IQR. For each outlier value-group, examine stroke prevalence in that group (or among others in group). If stroke prevalence is high (>= 50%), do not remove them as outliers. If moderately high (> 20%), try log2 transformation as a remedy and then re-evaluate outlier status using the same criteria? This is a bit fuzzy. \n# Another interpretation: after IQR flags outliers, we need to verify the stroke association condition. If for a flagged outlier value, the proportion of stroke cases among all observations sharing the same value is >= 0.5, then that value is not treated as an outlier. If proportion > 0.2 (i.e. 0.2 < prop < 0.5), then apply log2 transform to avg_glucose_level and redo the entire outlier detection process? Or just redo the check? \n# Also note \"the above check should be performed again\" - \"above check\" could refer to checking the association with stroke (the 50% threshold check) after transformation. \n# Let us look at the values we see: most outlier values appear only once. For singleton values, \"other observations sharing the same values\" = 0 observations. So fraction is 0/0? Or 0%. In that case, neither condition holds (not >= 50%, not > 20%). So they remain outliers. That fits many of the values. \n# The value 277.28 appears twice (n=2 total), stroke_prop among same-value obs is 0.5 (1 stroke out of 2). So \"other observations sharing the same values\" for each of those 2 observations - if you pick one, the other is the \"other observation\". So among \"other observations\", how many correspond to stroke? For one observation that is stroke case, the other is non-stroke: so among other observations (n=1), stroke cases = 0, so 0% of other observations correspond to stroke. For the other observation that is non-stroke, the \"other observation\" is stroke case: so among other observations, 100% correspond to stroke. \n# That gives different results per instance. That changes everything.\n\nSo the rule applies \"For observations...\" (each observation individually). For a given observation O with value v = avg_glucose_level, look at all other observations sharing the same value v. Let S be the set of other observations (same v, excluding O). Let f be fraction of S with stroke==1. Then: if f >= 0.5, O is not considered an outlier. If f > 0.2, apply log2 transformation and perform the above check again? Or the rule as written: \"If more than 20% of other observations sharing the same values correspond to stroke cases, a base-2 logarithmic transformation should be applied and the above check should be performed again.\" - \"the above check\" is the check about whether 50% or more of other observations... correspond to stroke cases? So after log2 transform, we re-check the same condition (>=50% of other observations sharing same values correspond to stroke)? That’s odd. Or maybe after log2, we redo outlier detection on the transformed variable and then reapply this rule? \n# This is ambiguous. Let us look for other clues in the full task. Also: \"Before making a final decision to reject outliers based on avg_glucose_level, verify whether this condition is associated with the occurrence of a stroke according to the rule.\" So the \"condition\" is being an outlier in avg_glucose_level. Verify association with stroke occurrence according to the rule given. The rule gives two branches. Also the second branch mentions applying log2 and performing \"the above check\" again. Maybe the \"above check\" is the stroke-association check? That is plausible. Let us think differently.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
