timestamp=2026-11-04T13:25:35.611Z level=INFO run=f66adc04 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Identify outliers in multidimensional space of numerical variables using Mahalanobis distance.\n# Consider observations for which the squared distance exceeds the critical value of the chi-square distribution to be outliers.\n# The critical value for the chi-square distribution should be set to 0.01.\n# For Mahalanobis, we need the covariance matrix. Usually use empirical covariance (or robust). Also how many degrees of freedom?\n# For p variables, df = p. Here p = 3 (age, bmi, avg_glucose_level). Critical value at alpha=0.01 with df=3.\n# Chi-square critical value at 0.01, df=3 is about 11.3449.\n\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import chi2\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/synthetic_stroke_data.csv')\nmask_valid = (df['avg_glucose_level'] >= 20) & (df['avg_glucose_level'] <= 800) & \\\n             (df['bmi'] >= 10) & (df['bmi'] <= 60) & \\\n             (df['age'] <= 120)\ndf_valid = df[mask_valid].copy()\ndf_valid = df_valid.reset_index(drop=True)\n\n# remove standard outliers first (as done above) before computing Mahalanobis on what data?\n# The instruction says: \"Remove standard outliers from the data, and then create a risk proxy variable...\" and then\n# \"Identify outliers in the multidimensional space of numerical variables using the Mahalanobis distance...\" on what data? Probably on the data after removing standard outliers.\n# That makes sense - we work with the cleaned dataset.\n\noutliers_to_remove_std = set()\nfor col in ['age', 'bmi']:\n    q1 = df_valid[col].quantile(0.25); q3 = df_valid[col].quantile(0.75); iqr=q3-q1\n    for idx in df_valid[(df_valid[col]<q1-3*iqr)|(df_valid[col]>q3+3*iqr)].index:\n        outliers_to_remove_std.add(idx)\ncol = 'avg_glucose_level'\nq1 = df_valid[col].quantile(0.25); q3 = df_valid[col].quantile(0.75); iqr=q3-q1\nglc_out = df_valid[(df_valid[col]<q1-3*iqr)|(df_valid[col]>q3+3*iqr)].index\nfor idx in glc_out:\n    v = df_valid.at[idx,col]\n    g = df_valid[df_valid[col]==v]\n    p = g['stroke'].mean()\n    if p >= 0.5:\n        continue\n    elif p > 0.2:\n        if p >= 0.5: continue\n        outliers_to_remove_std.add(idx)\n    else:\n        outliers_to_remove_std.add(idx)\n\ndf_clean = df_valid.drop(index=list(outliers_to_remove_std)).copy()\ndf_clean = df_clean.reset_index(drop=True)\n\n# compute Mahalanobis on numerical vars: age, bmi, avg_glucose_level\nnum_cols = ['age', 'bmi', 'avg_glucose_level']\nX = df_clean[num_cols].values\n\n# mean and covariance\nmean = np.mean(X, axis=0)\ncov = np.cov(X, rowvar=False)\ninv_cov = np.linalg.pinv(cov)\n\n# compute distances\nmahal = []\nfor i in range(X.shape[0]):\n    diff = X[i] - mean\n    d2 = diff @ inv_cov @ diff\n    mahal.append(d2)\n\n# critical value chi2 at 0.01, df = number of vars\nalpha = 0.01\ndf_chi = len(num_cols)  # 3\ncrit = chi2.ppf(1 - alpha, df=df_chi)  # or just use alpha as given? \"critical value for the chi-square distribution should be set to 0.01\"\n# wait: \"The critical value for the chi-square distribution should be set to 0.01.\"\n# That wording is odd. Usually we say significance level alpha = 0.01. Or sometimes they just give the critical value number. But the instruction literally says \"critical value ... should be set to 0.01\". That might mean use 0.01 as the critical value threshold (i.e. if squared Mahalanobis > 0.01, consider it outlier)? That’s unusual because chi-square critical values are larger (e.g., df=3 -> 11.34 at alpha=0.01). Let us check the exact instruction again.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
