timestamp=2026-11-04T13:31:45.901Z level=INFO run=f66adc04 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Now categorical outliers\n# \"In the final step, you should also check whether there are any values among the categorical variables that are unlikely in reality,\n# such as “smoker” or “formerly smoked” / person working in children. Additionaly you should chceck\n# whether there are any toddlers that are students. If so they should be also considered as outliers.\"\n# Look at categorical vars: gender, ever_married, work_type, residence_type, smoking_status\n# Also age-based conditions: \"For outlier detection in categorical variables, the age of children should be set to no more than 14.\n# For outlier detection in categorical variables, the age of toddelrs should be set to no more than 3.\"\n# So children age <= 14, toddlers age <= 3. And check combinations: toddlers that are students - outliers. \n# Also \"smoker\" or \"formerly smoked\" / person working in children\" - the phrasing is a bit odd, maybe \"smoker or formerly smoked person working in children\" (child labor / inappropriate work combination)? Or maybe \"smoker or formerly smoked\" combined with certain categories? Or just unusual combinations like work_type \"children\" but age > 14? Or the text as written is ambiguous. \n# Another reading: \"values among the categorical variables that are unlikely in reality, such as 'smoker' or 'formerly smoked' / person working in children.\" Maybe \"smoker\" and \"formerly smoked\" are normal; but \"person working in children\" (work_type children?) is problematic? Or maybe slash separates examples: e.g. \"smoker\" (unusual alone?) or combinations like \"formerly smoked\" person who is/was working with children? Or more directly, check for contradictions like: work_type is \"children\" but the person is not a child? Or age criteria given: children age <= 14, toddlers <= 3. So \"person working in children\" - maybe work_type is 'children'? That doesn’t make sense as an occupation category in typical datasets (sometimes work_type is children, meaning they’re children/not working). Also \"toddlers that are students\" - if age <= 3 and work_type/student status implies student, that’s unlikely. Let us look at the actual values.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
