timestamp=2026-11-04T13:32:02.080Z level=INFO run=f66adc04 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So work_type has 'children'. Also 'student'. \n# \"For outlier detection in categorical variables, the age of children should be set to no more than 14. For outlier detection in categorical variables, the age of toddelrs should be set to no more than 3.\"\n# \"whether there are any toddlers that are students. If so they should be also considered as outliers.\"\n# Toddler: age <= 3. Student means work_type 'student'? Or just student status. Look at data: work_type includes 'student' and 'children'. So \"students\" likely means work_type == 'student'. So any row with age <= 3 and work_type == 'student' is an outlier.\n# Also \"smoker\" or \"formerly smoked\" / person working in children\" - maybe work_type is 'children' but age > 14? Or the example given is confusing. Another interpretation: if someone is working in \"children\" category (work_type 'children') but they are an adult? Or if age > 14 and work_type is 'children', that’s odd. Or if age <= 14, work_type 'children' is normal. Also for children (age <= 14), they might be 'children' or 'student' depending. For toddlers (age <= 3), are they 'children' in work_type? Usually 'children' means not in workforce/child status. Toddlers being students is odd.\n# Let us look at actual combinations.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
