timestamp=2026-11-04T13:33:43.615Z level=INFO run=f66adc04 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Also toddlers who are students (work_type student and age <= 3) - we saw 1 case. That’s explicitly mentioned.\n# So both cases exist. These are categorical/numeric consistency issues. They should be considered outliers according to the instruction.\n# Now collect all these outlier indices (original indices).\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/synthetic_stroke_data.csv')\ncat_outliers = set()\n\n# toddlers that are students: age <= 3 and work_type == 'student'\nmask = (df['age'] <= 3) & (df['work_type'] == 'student')\nfor idx in df[mask].index:\n    cat_outliers.add(idx)\n\n# Also children (age <= 14) who smoke/formerly smoked? That’s very unusual. The instruction says \"values among the categorical variables that are unlikely in reality, such as “smoker” or “formerly smoked” / person working in children.\" \n# The slash could mean combinations: e.g. \"smoker or formerly smoked person\" AND \"person working in children\" (work_type children but they’re smokers?) - or maybe the example is just pointing to odd categories. But given the concrete examples in the task, toddlers who are students is explicitly stated. Also the age thresholds given for categorical outlier detection suggest age-category mismatches. \n# Another possibility: for categorical outlier detection, consider that if someone is in work_type 'children', that’s only valid for age <= 14 (children). Also toddlers are age <= 3. Maybe any row where work_type == 'children' but age > 14? Let us check if any. Or age <= 14 is considered children; age <= 3 toddlers. Also work_type 'children' matches children. That’s consistent. What about work_type 'student' but age is very young (toddler <= 3)? That’s the explicit case we found - already included.\n# Also \"smoker\" or \"formerly smoked\" in connection with \"children\" work type? Or maybe the instruction is saying: look for values like smoking-related categories that are odd in context of children? For example, children (age <= 14) with smoking_status 'smokes' or 'formerly smoked'. That is plausible. Also toddlers (age <= 3) smoking is definitely impossible. That’s also an outlier. Should we include those?\n# The instruction as written is a bit vague. But these are clearly data quality issues. Let us include both - toddlers who smoke (age <= 3 and smoking_status in {smokes, formerly smoked}) and children (age <= 14) who smoke/formerly smoked. Also maybe work_type is 'children' but age > 14? Check.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
